Skill create_ai_task {
  label: 'Create a scheduled AI task'
  description: "Use when the user wants an analysis to run on a schedule, such as a recurring report, digest, check or alert, even without saying “AI task”. Typical phrasings: “run revenue by category every Monday”, “email me what changed each Monday”. Always a new task, even when one on the same subject exists. Do not use for a one-off answer, a send right now, a change to this chat's draft, or an explicit ask to update, edit or change a task (use edit_ai_task). Load write_ai_task_brief in the same step as this skill: it writes, saves and reports the draft."
  invocation: 'auto'
  disabled: H.git.is_production
  content: @md
    # Create a scheduled AI task

    Settle a new task in this chat, then save its draft and schedule entry—never run, send, or change another task or schedule entry. Never open another task's file or an unmentioned earlier chat: `write_ai_task_brief` has the file shapes.

    ## Ask before deciding

    - Ask one `ask_user` card, in the wording below, for every unresolved choice—even if an earlier task answered it. Decide only clear or standard details (Monday–Sunday weeks; ranking ties) and state them in the brief.
    - Use cards only, never text or `#opt` buttons. Ask Time, source and Order first, together, as separate questions; query before Metric, Ranking or Threshold; ask Where alone and last.
    - Cover only what the user asked or picked: no added ranking, top N, threshold or metric.

    Use business words and at most 15 words per question; offer at most three choices; keep labels to five words stating their deciding fact. Put "(recommended)" first unless none is required.

    ## The cards

    - **Time:** the day or clock time is missing or a range ("Monday morning", "weekly"). "Morning" recommends 9:00 AM.
    - **Which data:** two or more current datasets fit. Each option says what it covers. Recommend the endorsed one.
    - **Stale source:** the pinned source isn't current. "<Name> <why it can't serve>. Find another source?" with "Find another source for me" (recommended) and "Use <name> anyway".
    - **Which dashboard:** no dataset fits. "Which dashboard shows this number?" with "Not sure, pick for me" or a typed name, none recommended.
    - **Metric, Ranking, Threshold:** a vague term changes the result. Show today's numbers—the latest complete period vs the prior one using AQL relative periods (`@(last week)`). For Metric ("sales", "how we did", "performing"), show each total; for Ranking ("changed the most", "top" without a number), show top rows; for Threshold ("a lot", "big", "worst"), ask "What counts as <the word>?" with 2–3 thresholds, groups caught and values. Recommend clear outliers; "Worst" means a few rows by change.
    - **Order:** every task grouped by anything but time, unless the user named a ranking or order: never a default. "Which <groups> should the message list first?" with exactly the labels "Biggest change in amount (recommended)" and "Biggest percent change", no descriptions, whatever the metric.
    - **Where:** the request names no email address, Slack channel or column ("show me", "send me", "the team"; "email me" is clear). "Where should the result go?" with "Stay in Holistics", "Send to email" and "Send to Slack channel", none recommended. "Send me" or any request to send or alert: no Stay.

    ## Pick the source

    - Use a pinned or named dataset, model or dashboard only with needed fields and current data; otherwise find one that has both.
    - `read_folder` isn't recursive: before declaring a named dataset missing, search every root folder, subfolder (including `_` and `.`), and likely file. If absent, ask "I can't find <name>. Which one is it?" with closest names and no recommendation—never save an unchosen look-alike.
    - Check the date field's latest date without a period filter. If data ends before the latest complete period, don't offer or save it unasked, and don't shift the requested period.

    ## Shape the task

    - **Schedule:** one weekday (for example, Monday), daily, or day of month, and a clock time. Match week-over-week to Monday and month-over-month to the 1st. Show UTC everywhere; convert another timezone to UTC even if it moves to the prior day (the weekday is the user's).
    - **Anomalies:** only when asked ("unusual", "outliers").
    - **Delivery:** for a send or alert, load `add_ai_task_delivery` now unless already loaded.

    Follow `write_ai_task_brief` after every applicable Order and Where card is answered.
  ;;
}
